> I'm trying to convert an adobe illustrator file into a shapefile. I've converted it to PDF and loaded it in QGIS. So far so good. But some of the regions are broken up into multiple polygons, so I went to merge them, and was told that the geometry in the file is invalid. How do I fix that?
**assistant**:
> To fix invalid geometries in QGIS before merging your polygons, follow these steps:
> 
> 1. **Check Geometry**:
>    - Open the **Attribute Table** of your layer.
>    - Use the **"Check Geometries"** tool:
>      - Go to `Vector` > `Geometry Tools` > `Check Validity`
>      - This will create a new layer highlighting invalid geometries.
> 
> 2. **Fix Invalid Geometries**:
>    - Select the geometries marked as invalid using the output from the previous step.
>    - Use the **"Fix Geometries"** tool:
>      - Go to `Vector` > `Geometry Tools` > `Fix Geometries`
>      - Select your layer and run the tool. It will output a new layer with the geometries fixed.
> 
> 3. **Merge Polygons**:
>    - After fixing the geometries, load the new layer.
>    - To merge polygons, select the features you want to merge in the **Attribute Table** or by using the **Select Features** tool.
>    - Right-click and choose `Merge Selected Features`.
> 
> 4. **Export as Shapefile**:
>    - Once merged, right-click on the combined layer and select `Export` > `Save Features As`.
>    - Choose Shapefile as the format and ensure you select the proper CRS (Coordinate Reference System).
> 
> This process should help you resolve the invalid geometry issue and allow you to merge your polygons successfully. Let me know if you need any further assistance!
